  "step_2": "The individual states that winning a relatively high sum in Lottery A is more probable than in Lottery B — this reflects a focus on the probability of the favorable outcome (3000 vs. 4000 Euros). The individual notes that although Lottery B offers a higher potential sum (4000 Euros), they do not find the risk worth taking. This indicates that the individual is weighing the probability of winning a high sum against the risk of getting nothing. The deliberation centers on the likelihood of a favorable outcome and the perceived value of taking risk. There is no mention of the distribution of probabilities around zero (i.e., the skewness), nor any reference to the proportion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes. The justification is based on expected value or risk aversion in terms of probability of winning, not on skewness as defined in the decision reason. The individual does not compare the degree of right-skewness (i.e., the relative weight of 0 Euro outcomes) between the two lotteries, nor does they refer to unfavorable outcomes as dominating in one lottery over the other.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — namely, the degree of right-skewness (proportion of probability on 0 Euros) — is not considered by the individual. The individual focuses instead on the probability of winning a high sum (favorable outcome), which is a different aspect of the decision. The decision reason requires comparing the skewness (favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes), specifically the greater weight on unfavorable outcomes. The individual’s justification does not mention or evaluate the probability of zero outcomes or the imbalance in the distribution toward the lower end. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The reasoning is based on a different principle (e.g., probability of winning a large sum), not on skewness. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
