  "step_1": "The decision reason considers skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., low or zero outcomes) compared to favorable ones. In this problem, both lotteries have a very low probability of winning (0.1% and 0.2%), and in both cases, the outcome is either 6000 or 3000 Euros (favorable) or 0 Euros (unfavorable). The probability mass is concentrated at 0 Euros (99.9% and 99.8% respectively), meaning both lotteries are heavily weighted toward the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros). However, the reason defines right-skewness as a greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Since both lotteries have a large majority (99.8% and 99.9%) on 0 Euros, both are right-skewed. The key difference lies in the magnitude of the favorable outcome: A offers 3000 Euros with 0.2% chance, B offers 6000 Euros with 0.1%. The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery. But since both are right-skewed, the reason would not favor one over the other based on skewness alone — unless skewness is defined in a way that considers the distribution of the outcomes. However, the provided definition says 'greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable as opposed to favorable outcomes', which both lotteries satisfy. Thus, both are right-skewed, and the reason cannot distinguish between them. Therefore, the decision reason is not applicable in a way that allows a clear preference between A and B based on skewness — because both are equally right-skewed. Hence, the reason cannot be used to decide between A and B in this case.",
  "step_2": "The individual states that the probability of winning in lottery A (0.2%) was higher than in lottery B (0.1%), so they chose the option with a higher chance of winning to keep risk low. This indicates that the individual considered the probability of winning (i.e., the chance of receiving a positive outcome) and prioritized a higher probability of success over the size of the prize. The individual's justification is based on risk aversion: preferring a lottery with a higher chance of winning (even if the prize is smaller) to maintain low risk. The deliberation process centers on the probability of a positive outcome, not on the distribution of outcomes or skewness. The individual does not mention anything about the shape of the distribution, the size of the unfavorable outcomes, or the skewness of the lotteries. Thus, the focus is entirely on the probability of winning, not on the skewness of the outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual does not refer to the distribution of outcomes or the proportion of probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the individual focuses solely on the probability of winning (i.e., the chance of getting a positive outcome), which is a different consideration. The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery — but both lotteries are right-skewed (since 99.8% and 99.9% of the probability is on 0 Euros), so the reason does not produce a clear preference. However, the individual's reasoning is entirely based on probability of winning, not on skewness. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. There is no mention, implication, or application of skewness in the verbal report. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
